    "text_full": "Skip to content\nAbout Us\nPrograms\nNews & Media\nDONATE\nFR\nHERITAGE MINUTES\nPODCASTS\nVIDEOS\nEDUCATION GUIDES\nSEARCH PRODUCTIONS\nHome\n / Videos / Canadian Innovations\nCanadian Innovations\n\nCanadian innovations have helped shaped the world we live in today. Learn more about these innovations, and the Canadian inventors behind them, with Historica Canada’s video series, produced in partnership with the Rideau Hall Foundation\n\nFeatures\nOlivia Poole: The Ojibwe woman who invented the baby jumper\nPopular\nOlivia Poole invented the Jolly Jumper for her children in 1910.\nFoghorn: the lifesaving Canadian invention no one patented or profited from\nFeatured\nThe world’s first steam-operated fog alarm was installed on Partridge Island in 1859.\nAll (4) Episodes\nSort by:\nDate\nA to Z\nSearch & Filter\nOlivia Poole: The Ojibwe woman who invented the baby jumper\n2020\nConverto-Braille: the innovation that opened up a new world for the visually impaired\n2020\nDr. Maude Abbott: the Canadian woman who paved the way for modern heart surgery\n2020\nFoghorn: the lifesaving Canadian invention no one patented or profited from\n2020\n\nHistorica Canada is a charitable organization that offers programs in both official languages that you can use to explore, learn, reflect on our history, and consider what it means to be Canadian.\n\nHistorica Canada's business number is 885434720RR0001\n\nAbout Us\nPolicies\nCareers\nHistorica Canada Content Usage Request Form\n\nFollow Us\nMore From Historica Canada\nCitizenship Challenge\nHeritage Minutes\nThe Memory Project\nThe Canadian Encyclopedia\nPartnership: The World Remembers\n© Historica Canada 2026. All rights reserved.\nWebsite by DS"
